awled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.08980\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 168\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 18 Apr 2019]\nL9: # Title:Exploring the Limitations of Behavior Cloning for Autonomous Driving\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Felipe Codevilla , cite6†Eder Santana , cite7†Antonio M. López , cite8†Adrien Gaidon L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Exploring the Limitations of Behavior Cloning for Autonomous Driving, by Felipe Codevilla and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Driving requires reacting to a wide variety of complex environment conditions and agent behaviors. Explicitly modeling each possible scenario is unrealistic. In contrast, imitation learning can, in theory, leverage data from large fleets of human-driven cars. Behavior cloning in particular has been successfully used to learn simple visuomotor policies end-to-end, but scaling to the full spectrum of driving behaviors remains an unsolved problem.\nL17: In this paper, we propose a new benchmark to experimentally investigate the scalability and limitations of behavior cloning. We show that behavior cloning leads to state-of-the-art results, including in unseen environments, executing complex lateral and longitudinal maneuvers without these reactions being explicitly programmed.\nL18: However, we confirm well-known limitations (due to dataset bias and overfitting), new generalization issues (due to dynamic objects and the lack of a causal model), and training instability requiring further research before behavior cloning can graduate to real-world driving. The code of the studied behavior cloning approaches can be found at cite11†this https URL†github.com .\nL19: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:1904.08980 [cs.CV]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:1904.08980v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1904.08980†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Felipe Codevilla [cite15†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Thu, 18 Apr 2019 19:29:56 UTC (9,330 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Exploring the Limitations of Behavior Cloning for Autonomous Driving, by Felipe Codevilla and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite9†View PDF L35:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite16†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite17†view license L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CV\nL42: \nL43: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L44: \nL45: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2019-04 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite23†cs L50: cite24†cs.AI L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: ### cite28†DBLP†dblp.uni-trier.de - CS Bibliography\nL58: \nL59: cite29†listing†dblp.uni-trier.de | cite30†bibtex†dblp.uni-trier.de L60: \nL61: cite31†Felipe Codevilla†dblp.uni-trier.de L62: cite32†Eder Santana†dblp.uni-trier.de L63: cite33†Antonio M. López†dblp.uni-trier.de L64: cite34†Adrien Gaidon†dblp.uni-trier.de L65: \nL66: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL67: \nL68: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL69: \nL70: [Button: ×]\nL71: \nL72: loading...\nL73: \nL74: Data provided by:\nL75: \nL76: ### Bookmark\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL79: # Bibliographic and Citat